# At the Very Same Place!

On hearing of "Hindustan Nagar" in Thyagaraya Nagar and being directed to go by way of Thanikachalam Chetty Road, one even feared that Thyagaraya Nagar itself might be transformed.

The pomp, newspaper reports, and vast crowd keeping rhythm for the devotional singing must have bewildered everyone. Many thoughts arose: were such events intended to obstruct our growth? Were they spectacles conducted before an election? Or were they acts designed not to end but to conceal the Kamaraj-Rajaji dispute?

When we heard speakers at Hindustan Nagar say that everyone must speak and practise Hindustani, another struggle arose in the mind: were preparations already being made to impose Hindi as a compulsory subject if Congress came to power?

At intervals we heard that Rajaji did not know Hindi, that Kamaraj did not know Hindi, and that when the president of the Hindi Prachar Sabha stumbled through the one sentence he had memorised, everyone, including Gandhi, laughed. It causes us wonder.

The news that brings a smile is that our labour and effort concerning the question of nationality have not been wasted. The issue was not manufactured by our labour and effort; the national question lies embedded within the country.

At that very place, while Gandhi's words about Hindi still rang in the ears and devotional songs split the heart, another conference was reportedly held. Its name was the "Conference of Writers in the Dravidian Languages." Not only the name, but the speech of the presiding Kannada writer, Masti Venkatesa Iyengar, produced astonishment and bewilderment beyond measure.

He said:

> "India has approximately thirteen or twenty major languages. People should acquire familiarity with as many of them as possible. I doubt whether everyone in South India can learn Hindi. But the Prachar Sabha's objective should be to enable as many people as possible to learn it.
>
> "Those who wish to maintain contact with North India should learn Hindi. At the same time, north Indians who wish to maintain contact with the South should learn one or two Dravidian languages. People in the South should seek to establish in North India a Prachar Sabha for one of the four Dravidian languages."

Viewed through the same national lens, what a beneficial speech! National awakening is sprouting not only in Tamil Nadu but throughout Dravidam. Whether it is Hindustan or Baniastan, the national question comes forward and is given a place.

We commend our Kannada writer-comrade Masti for his clear ideas. He doubts whether the people of the South can be made to learn Hindi. And where did he stand when he expressed that doubt? At the silver-jubilee celebration of the Hindi Prachar Sabha.

Whether he discussed the reasons for his doubt we do not know; this is all that appeared in the newspapers. He also said that those who wished might learn it. Was it not for saying precisely this that Thalamuthu and Natarajan were sent from prison to the grave and mothers were reviled?

Comrade Masti did not stop there. He says those who desire contact with the North may study Hindi. Further, he points a rebuking finger at north Indians: "If you desire contact with the South, you must learn one or two Dravidian languages." Going still further, he calls for a Dravidian-language Prachar Sabha in North India.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 19-2-46)
